WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.700
vLLM.

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.077
Serving a language model to many users at once is a memory problem first.

3
00:00:07.077 --> 00:00:09.233
vLLM is the open source answer.

4
00:00:10.433 --> 00:00:14.467
vLLM is the open source standard for serving language models.

5
00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:18.767
Each generating sequence keeps an attention memory, the KV cache.

6
00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:25.000
Its trick, PagedAttention, manages that memory like an operating system manages RAM.

7
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.600
Let's compare two ways to reserve that memory.

8
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.843
Naive serving gives each request one big contiguous slab.

9
00:00:32.843 --> 00:00:35.333
Paging uses small blocks, on demand.

10
00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:38.349
The slab is sized for the maximum length.

11
00:00:38.349 --> 00:00:40.667
Paging grows only as tokens appear.

12
00:00:40.967 --> 00:00:44.500
So naive wastes sixty to eighty percent, paging about four.

13
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.333
Far more sequences fit on one GPU.

14
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:50.100
Here is how paging works.

15
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.767
Each request is a sequence of tokens.

16
00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:55.367
A block table tracks which blocks it owns.

17
00:00:55.667 --> 00:00:58.133
The blocks can sit anywhere in GPU memory.

18
00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:02.633
More resident sequences enable continuous batching.

19
00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:06.233
The GPU decodes one token for every live sequence.

20
00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:08.333
Finished sequences leave right away.

21
00:01:08.633 --> 00:01:12.533
Queued requests join, so the GPU never waits for a whole batch.

22
00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:15.833
Learn the metrics vocabulary cold.

23
00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:20.167
TTFT is time to first token: how fast a reply starts.

24
00:01:20.467 --> 00:01:24.067
TPOT is time per output token: how fast it streams.

25
00:01:24.367 --> 00:01:27.900
Throughput is total tokens per second, what you provision by.

26
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.300
Here it is in code.

27
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.367
One vllm serve command starts an OpenAI compatible endpoint.

28
00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:39.367
The ordinary OpenAI client just points at your own machine.

29
00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:45.133
One user gets forty two tokens a second; thirty two users get about eleven hundred fifty.

30
00:01:46.333 --> 00:01:47.800
Here is where it pays off.

31
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.233
Your fine-tuned summarizer must serve real users.

32
00:01:51.533 --> 00:01:56.267
One command on an A10G, and the backend only swaps the base URL.

33
00:01:56.567 --> 00:01:59.767
Time to first token stays under four hundred milliseconds.

34
00:02:00.967 --> 00:02:01.817
Let's recap.

35
00:02:01.817 --> 00:02:05.500
PagedAttention pages the KV cache into small blocks.

36
00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:08.567
Continuous batching keeps the GPU busy.

37
00:02:08.867 --> 00:02:11.433
Know TTFT, TPOT, and throughput.

38
00:02:11.733 --> 00:02:15.583
And one command gives you an OpenAI style endpoint.

39
00:02:15.583 --> 00:02:16.867
Next up: LiteLLM.
